---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ResponsiveImage from '../../components/ResponsiveImage.astro';
import { about, pillars } from '../../data/gallery.js';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Trevor Ludwyck is a photographer based in Celina, TX, working throughout Dallas–Fort Worth. Oklahoma-raised, Oklahoma State journalism grad, with a background photographing for nonprofit and missions organizations around the world."
>
  <section class="about wrap">
    <div class="about__image">
      <ResponsiveImage src={about.src} alt={about.alt} sizes="(min-width: 900px) 40vw, 100vw" />
    </div>
    <div class="about__content">
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>Less is more.</h1>
      <p>
        I've always been drawn to the moments that don't need much
        explanation.
      </p>
      <p>
        My first experience with a camera came when I was young,
        photographing wildlife on hunting trips with my dad in Oklahoma.
        Years later, I studied journalism at Oklahoma State University,
        where I learned to look for the story behind the image. Photography
        became a natural extension of that.
      </p>
      <p>
        Since then, I've had the opportunity to travel around the world
        photographing for nonprofit and missions organizations, documenting
        events, creating portraits, and telling stories of people from all
        different walks of life. Those experiences have shaped the way I
        see photography. I've learned that no matter where you are or what
        your story looks like, there is something worth capturing in every
        person.
      </p>
      <p>
        Today, I live in Celina, Texas, with my wife, Amy. We attend
        Shoreline City Church, and are honored to be a small part of what
        God is doing there. I spend a lot of my life around people,
        stories, and moments that matter — and photography has become one
        of my favorite ways to preserve them.
      </p>
      <p>
        I'm not interested in making photographs feel overly complicated. I
        want them to feel intentional, honest, and timeless. Whether it's a
        portrait, an engagement, a milestone, or an ordinary moment worth
        remembering.
      </p>
      <p>
        For me, the best photographs don't just show you what something
        looked like. They remind you what it felt like to be there.
      </p>
      <a href="/contact/" class="btn">Inquire</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="pillars wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">Philosophy</p>
    <h2>My Three Pillars</h2>
    <p class="pillars__intro">
      Vision changes how you approach everything — including a camera.
      These are the three ideas that shape the way I see, and the way I
      shoot.
    </p>

    <div class="pillars__list">
      <details class="pillar">
        <summary class="pillar__summary">
          <span class="pillar__name">Light</span>
          <span class="pillar__icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </summary>
        <div class="pillar__content">
          <ResponsiveImage
            src={pillars.light.src}
            alt={pillars.light.alt}
            sizes="(min-width: 700px) 500px, 100vw"
            class="pillar__image"
          />
          <div class="pillar__text">
            <p class="pillar__verse">
              Genesis 1:3 — "And God said, 'Let there be light,' and there
              was light."
            </p>
            <p>
              Light is the first thing God ever created. It is the
              fundamental element to all of life. Photography is more than
              just taking photos. It's about capturing and shaping light to
              narrate a story, freezing it in time.
            </p>
            <p>
              Where the light falls, what it reveals, what it leaves in
              shadow... that's the actual craft. The camera, the lens, the
              location: all of it just serves the light.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="pillar">
        <summary class="pillar__summary">
          <span class="pillar__name">Contrast</span>
          <span class="pillar__icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </summary>
        <div class="pillar__content">
          <ResponsiveImage
            src={pillars.contrast.src}
            alt={pillars.contrast.alt}
            sizes="(min-width: 700px) 500px, 100vw"
            class={pillars.contrast.grayscale ? 'pillar__image pillar__image--grayscale' : 'pillar__image'}
          />
          <div class="pillar__text">
            <p>
              Contrast is the key ingredient to quality. An artist uses
              contrasting colors to add life to a painting. A dish needs
              something acidic against something rich, or sweet against
              salt, to actually taste like anything. A scent falls flat
              without a top note working against a base note.
            </p>
            <p>
              Photography runs on the same rule. Without contrast — in
              light, in tone, in composition — images fall flat. Contrast
              is what gives photography depth, weight, and a reason to
              look twice.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="pillar">
        <summary class="pillar__summary">
          <span class="pillar__name">Simplicity</span>
          <span class="pillar__icon" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </summary>
        <div class="pillar__content">
          <ResponsiveImage
            src={pillars.simplicity.src}
            alt={pillars.simplicity.alt}
            sizes="(min-width: 700px) 500px, 100vw"
            class="pillar__image"
          />
          <div class="pillar__text">
            <p>
              I believe true skill is finding the easiest, most efficient
              way to complete a task. But that does not mean making the
              task itself easy. Take CrossFit: the best athletes in the
              world aren't the best because they've figured out how to
              make CrossFit easy. They've found the most efficient way to
              do something that's still genuinely hard.
            </p>
            <p>
              This is my approach to photography. Less is always more —
              fewer props, fewer poses, fewer distractions. The skill was
              never in the complexity. It's in knowing exactly what to
              leave out.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about {
    padding-block: calc(var(--space-xl) + 2rem) var(--space-xl);
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 5fr 7fr;
    gap: var(--space-lg);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .about__image :global(img) {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 5;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  .about__content h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2.25rem, 4.5vw, 3rem);
    margin-block: var(--space-sm) var(--space-md);
  }

  .about__content p {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
    max-width: 58ch;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
  }

  .about__content .btn {
    margin-top: var(--space-sm);
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .about {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }

  .pillars {
    padding-top: var(--space-xl);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-xl);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .pillars h2 {
    font-size: clamp(1.85rem, 3.5vw, 2.5rem);
    margin-block: var(--space-sm) var(--space-md);
  }

  .pillars__intro {
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    max-width: 60ch;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-lg);
  }

  .pillars__list {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .pillar {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
  }

  .pillar__summary {
    list-style: none;
    cursor: pointer;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--space-md);
    padding-block: var(--space-md);
  }

  .pillar__summary::-webkit-details-marker {
    display: none;
  }

  .pillar__summary::marker {
    content: '';
  }

  .pillar__name {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 3.5vw, 2.25rem);
  }

  .pillar__icon {
    position: relative;
    width: 18px;
    height: 18px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  .pillar__icon::before,
  .pillar__icon::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    background: var(--color-gold);
  }

  .pillar__icon::before {
    top: 50%;
    left: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 1px;
    transform: translateY(-50%);
  }

  .pillar__icon::after {
    left: 50%;
    top: 0;
    width: 1px;
    height: 100%;
    transform: translateX(-50%);
    transition: opacity 0.15s ease;
  }

  details[open] .pillar__icon::after {
    opacity: 0;
  }

  .pillar__content {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 5fr 7fr;
    gap: var(--space-lg);
    align-items: start;
    padding-bottom: var(--space-lg);
  }

  .pillar__content :global(img) {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 5;
    object-fit: cover;
  }

  .pillar__content :global(img.pillar__image--grayscale) {
    filter: grayscale(1);
  }

  .pillar__text {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
  }

  .pillar__verse {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    color: var(--color-text);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--color-gold);
    padding-left: var(--space-sm);
  }

  @media (max-width: 700px) {
    .pillar__content {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
